1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,170
On November twenty-first, 2026, after more than eight years in space,

2
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:08,840
a European and Japanese spacecraft fired its engine,

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:10,604
and let Mercury catch it.

4
00:00:11,104 --> 00:00:12,515
It's called BepiColombo.

5
00:00:12,515 --> 00:00:17,044
And it's only the second mission ever to orbit the planet closest to the Sun.

6
00:00:17,544 --> 00:00:19,239
So what if you'd been on board?

7
00:00:19,239 --> 00:00:22,246
What if you landed on Mercury, and tried to live there?

8
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,600
First, what actually happened.

9
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:34,271
The big engine burn began on the evening of November twenty-first, European time.

10
00:00:34,271 --> 00:00:38,541
Just after midnight, ESA's flight dynamics team confirmed it.

11
00:00:38,541 --> 00:00:41,411
BepiColombo had been captured into orbit.

12
00:00:41,911 --> 00:00:44,867
It's really two spacecraft stacked together.

13
00:00:44,867 --> 00:00:48,159
ESA's Mercury Planetary Orbiter, and Japan's Mio,

14
00:00:48,159 --> 00:00:51,047
built to study the planet's magnetic field.

15
00:00:51,047 --> 00:00:54,742
They're due to split apart on December ninth and tenth.

16
00:00:55,242 --> 00:00:57,944
Science is set to begin in April 2027.

17
00:00:57,944 --> 00:01:02,922
Until then, each orbiter still has to be steered into its final orbit,

18
00:01:02,922 --> 00:01:05,908
so what they'll discover is still unknown.

19
00:01:06,408 --> 00:01:09,831
Only one spacecraft has orbited Mercury before.

20
00:01:09,831 --> 00:01:13,181
NASA's MESSENGER circled it from 2011 to 2015,

21
00:01:13,181 --> 00:01:16,968
then ended its mission by crashing into the surface.

22
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:20,890
Here's a surprise.

23
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:22,496
Mercury is hard to reach.

24
00:01:22,496 --> 00:01:26,416
Not because it's far away, but because the Sun is in the way.

25
00:01:26,916 --> 00:01:30,940
Fall toward the Sun and you speed up, like a car rolling downhill.

26
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:35,755
To be captured by little Mercury, you have to shed a huge amount of that speed.

27
00:01:36,255 --> 00:01:37,970
According to some estimates,

28
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:41,522
it takes less energy to reach Pluto than to reach Mercury.

29
00:01:42,022 --> 00:01:45,724
So BepiColombo braked the clever way, with nine flybys.

30
00:01:45,724 --> 00:01:49,358
One of Earth, two of Venus, and six of Mercury itself.

31
00:01:49,358 --> 00:01:51,377
Each one stole a little speed.

32
00:01:51,877 --> 00:01:57,600
The mission is named after Italian scientist Giuseppe Colombo, nicknamed Bepi.

33
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:58,187
In 1970,

34
00:01:58,187 --> 00:02:04,570
he worked out how NASA's Mariner 10 could loop back and fly past Mercury a second time.

35
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:08,231
Now the rules.

36
00:02:08,231 --> 00:02:12,530
You ride down in a lander, with a heat-proof suit and a small base.

37
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:13,941
We'll ignore the cost.

38
00:02:13,941 --> 00:02:15,930
Your only job is to stay alive.

39
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:20,252
You touch down at dawn, right on the line between night and day.

40
00:02:20,252 --> 00:02:23,416
And the first thing you notice is how light you feel.

41
00:02:23,416 --> 00:02:27,238
Mercury's gravity is only about thirty-eight percent of Earth's.

42
00:02:27,238 --> 00:02:30,223
A ten kilogram backpack feels like less than four.

43
00:02:30,723 --> 00:02:31,885
Then you look up.

44
00:02:31,885 --> 00:02:34,278
The sky is black, even in daylight.

45
00:02:34,278 --> 00:02:36,397
Mercury has no real atmosphere.

46
00:02:36,397 --> 00:02:39,541
Just a thin haze of atoms called an exosphere,

47
00:02:39,541 --> 00:02:43,711
knocked off the ground by the solar wind and tiny meteorites.

48
00:02:44,211 --> 00:02:45,610
And the Sun is enormous.

49
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:49,339
It can look more than three times wider than it does from Earth,

50
00:02:49,339 --> 00:02:52,427
and its light can be as much as seven times brighter.

51
00:02:55,167 --> 00:02:58,175
In your first hour, the sunlight starts to bite.

52
00:02:58,175 --> 00:02:59,241
In full daylight,

53
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:03,817
Mercury's ground can reach about four hundred and thirty degrees Celsius.

54
00:03:03,817 --> 00:03:05,760
That's hot enough to melt lead.

55
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:10,211
But step into the shadow of a boulder, and it's a different world.

56
00:03:10,211 --> 00:03:13,264
With no air to carry heat around, shade stays cold.

57
00:03:13,264 --> 00:03:18,174
On the night side, the ground drops to about minus one hundred and eighty degrees.

58
00:03:18,674 --> 00:03:21,980
That's a swing of about six hundred degrees between day and night.

59
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,245
Luckily, you won't have to face it all at once.

60
00:03:25,245 --> 00:03:27,658
Because on Mercury, the Sun barely moves.

61
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:32,219
Mercury spins very slowly.

62
00:03:32,219 --> 00:03:35,226
One turn takes about fifty-nine Earth days.

63
00:03:35,226 --> 00:03:39,143
Meanwhile, it races around the Sun in just eighty-eight.

64
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:42,715
Put those together, and something strange happens.

65
00:03:42,715 --> 00:03:47,630
From one sunrise to the next takes about one hundred and seventy-six Earth days.

66
00:03:48,130 --> 00:03:51,686
So the line between night and day creeps across the ground.

67
00:03:51,686 --> 00:03:52,770
By our rough math,

68
00:03:52,770 --> 00:03:57,592
at the equator it moves at about three and a half kilometers an hour on average.

69
00:03:57,592 --> 00:03:59,520
That's slower than a brisk walk.

70
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:03,081
Which means, in theory, you could live on the move.

71
00:04:03,081 --> 00:04:06,503
Keep pace with that line, and you'd stay at dawn forever,

72
00:04:06,503 --> 00:04:10,345
never facing the full heat of noon or the deepest cold of night.

73
00:04:10,845 --> 00:04:13,483
In Kim Stanley Robinson's novel 2312,

74
00:04:13,483 --> 00:04:16,976
a whole city called Terminator does exactly that,

75
00:04:16,976 --> 00:04:21,467
crawling around Mercury on rails, just ahead of the rising Sun.

76
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:27,465
Stay put instead, and after eighty-eight Earth days,

77
00:04:27,465 --> 00:04:29,764
Mercury has gone once around the Sun.

78
00:04:29,764 --> 00:04:31,255
Your first year is over.

79
00:04:31,755 --> 00:04:33,472
But your first day isn't.

80
00:04:33,472 --> 00:04:37,661
Mercury spins three times for every two trips around the Sun.

81
00:04:37,661 --> 00:04:42,262
So one full day, sunrise to sunrise, lasts two whole Mercury years.

82
00:04:42,762 --> 00:04:46,732
Astronomers only worked this out in 1965, using radar.

83
00:04:46,732 --> 00:04:51,657
Before that, many thought one side of Mercury always faced the Sun.

84
00:04:52,157 --> 00:04:54,049
And Mercury has a party trick.

85
00:04:54,049 --> 00:04:57,833
Its orbit is stretched, so near its closest pass to the Sun,

86
00:04:57,833 --> 00:05:01,427
it moves so fast that its orbit briefly outruns its spin.

87
00:05:01,927 --> 00:05:05,554
From some places on the surface, the Sun rises, stops,

88
00:05:05,554 --> 00:05:08,710
sinks back below the horizon, then rises again.

89
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:09,851
A double sunrise.

90
00:05:09,851 --> 00:05:15,492
And at two spots on the equator, the noon Sun hangs almost still overhead for weeks.

91
00:05:18,233 --> 00:05:20,656
Before the Sun goes down, a quick favour.

92
00:05:20,656 --> 00:05:23,493
If you're enjoying this, tap like and subscribe.

93
00:05:23,493 --> 00:05:25,147
It really helps the channel.

94
00:05:25,647 --> 00:05:30,173
Around eighty-eight Earth days after your sunrise, the Sun finally sets.

95
00:05:30,173 --> 00:05:32,247
And the night lasts just as long.

96
00:05:32,247 --> 00:05:34,698
Roughly three Earth months of darkness.

97
00:05:35,198 --> 00:05:37,420
With no atmosphere to hold the heat,

98
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:39,889
the ground bleeds its warmth into space,

99
00:05:39,889 --> 00:05:43,406
down toward minus one hundred and eighty degrees Celsius.

100
00:05:43,906 --> 00:05:46,575
Your base now needs heaters, not coolers.

101
00:05:46,575 --> 00:05:51,913
On Mercury, whether you freeze or fry depends almost entirely on where the Sun is.

102
00:05:54,667 --> 00:05:58,480
After one full Mercury day, you'd probably want a better address.

103
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:00,769
And the best one might be at the poles.

104
00:06:01,269 --> 00:06:04,481
Mercury's axis is tilted only about two degrees.

105
00:06:04,481 --> 00:06:07,962
So near the poles, the Sun always skims the horizon,

106
00:06:07,962 --> 00:06:12,112
and the floors of some deep craters never see sunlight at all.

107
00:06:12,612 --> 00:06:16,907
In 2012, NASA's MESSENGER confirmed what radar had hinted at.

108
00:06:16,907 --> 00:06:19,302
Those dark craters hold water ice.

109
00:06:19,302 --> 00:06:21,626
On the planet closest to the Sun.

110
00:06:22,126 --> 00:06:26,273
One estimate put it at a hundred billion to a trillion tonnes.

111
00:06:26,273 --> 00:06:29,150
Enough, said lead scientist David Lawrence,

112
00:06:29,150 --> 00:06:32,093
to bury an area the size of Washington, D.C.

113
00:06:32,093 --> 00:06:34,769
under more than three kilometers of ice.

114
00:06:35,269 --> 00:06:41,138
Much of it is hidden under a dark layer that scientists think is made of organic compounds,

115
00:06:41,138 --> 00:06:43,912
probably delivered by comets and asteroids.

116
00:06:44,412 --> 00:06:47,487
For a settler, that ice could mean water to drink,

117
00:06:47,487 --> 00:06:49,639
oxygen to breathe and fuel to make.

118
00:06:49,639 --> 00:06:52,284
Right next to the endless power of the Sun.

119
00:06:55,033 --> 00:06:55,827
Now look down.

120
00:06:55,827 --> 00:06:58,320
Because the ground beneath you is shrinking.

121
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:00,391
Mercury is mostly metal.

122
00:07:00,391 --> 00:07:05,494
Its iron core reaches out to about eighty-five percent of the planet's radius.

123
00:07:05,494 --> 00:07:08,177
Picture a peach that is almost all stone.

124
00:07:08,177 --> 00:07:13,084
That's why it's the second densest planet in the solar system, after Earth.

125
00:07:13,584 --> 00:07:19,104
As that core slowly cooled over billions of years, the whole planet contracted.

126
00:07:19,104 --> 00:07:19,663
In 2014,

127
00:07:19,663 --> 00:07:26,440
scientists using MESSENGER's maps estimated its radius has shrunk by as much as seven kilometers.

128
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:30,618
The crust buckled into giant cliffs called lobate scarps.

129
00:07:30,618 --> 00:07:35,780
Some run for hundreds of kilometers, and rise up to about three kilometers high.

130
00:07:36,633 --> 00:07:39,063
That core still makes a magnetic field,

131
00:07:39,063 --> 00:07:43,299
but at the surface it's only about one percent as strong as Earth's.

132
00:07:43,299 --> 00:07:45,604
A weak shield against the solar wind.

133
00:07:46,104 --> 00:07:49,773
It even twists into what NASA calls magnetic tornadoes,

134
00:07:49,773 --> 00:07:53,709
that funnel hot particles from the Sun down to the surface.

135
00:07:53,709 --> 00:07:56,978
Your base would need serious radiation shielding.

136
00:07:59,733 --> 00:08:04,333
Stay a century, and you'd be living on the planet that helped prove Einstein right.

137
00:08:04,833 --> 00:08:08,831
Mercury's stretched orbit slowly swings around the Sun.

138
00:08:08,831 --> 00:08:09,413
In 1859,

139
00:08:09,413 --> 00:08:15,664
astronomer Urbain Le Verrier found that it swings a little too fast for Newton's laws.

140
00:08:16,164 --> 00:08:17,229
The gap was tiny.

141
00:08:17,229 --> 00:08:19,422
Forty-three arcseconds per century.

142
00:08:19,422 --> 00:08:24,683
By our rough math, that's about the width of a coin seen from a hundred meters away.

143
00:08:25,183 --> 00:08:28,848
Einstein's new theory of gravity, general relativity,

144
00:08:28,848 --> 00:08:31,268
predicted exactly that extra swing.

145
00:08:31,268 --> 00:08:34,655
Mercury's orbit became one of its first big wins.

146
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:36,820
And here's the twist.

147
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:39,774
Mercury is one of the hardest planets to reach.

148
00:08:39,774 --> 00:08:42,854
Yet on average, it's the closest planet to Earth.

149
00:08:43,354 --> 00:08:46,051
Venus comes nearest at its closest point.

150
00:08:46,051 --> 00:08:50,064
But it also spends long stretches on the far side of the Sun.

151
00:08:50,064 --> 00:08:54,867
Mercury never strays far from the Sun, so it never gets that far from us.

152
00:08:55,367 --> 00:09:00,822
In 2019, three engineers ran the numbers in the magazine Physics Today.

153
00:09:00,822 --> 00:09:04,741
On average, Mercury is the closest planet to Earth.

154
00:09:04,741 --> 00:09:08,660
And to every other planet in the solar system, too.

155
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:14,807
By their math, Mercury averages about 1.04 times the Earth-Sun distance from us.

156
00:09:14,807 --> 00:09:16,190
Venus, about 1.14.

157
00:09:16,690 --> 00:09:21,216
So our nearest neighbour, on average, took more than eight years to reach.

158
00:09:21,216 --> 00:09:22,746
And starting next spring,

159
00:09:22,746 --> 00:09:26,477
BepiColombo will study how this strange little planet formed,

160
00:09:26,477 --> 00:09:27,639
and how it changed.

161
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:32,561
So life on Mercury means a black sky, a Sun three times wider,

162
00:09:32,561 --> 00:09:34,330
a day that lasts two years,

163
00:09:34,330 --> 00:09:38,129
and ice hiding next to the hottest sunlight of any planet.

164
00:09:38,629 --> 00:09:40,988
If you moved there, where would you live?

165
00:09:40,988 --> 00:09:44,842
In the frozen craters at the poles, or chasing the sunrise forever?

166
00:09:44,842 --> 00:09:46,223
Tell me in the comments.
